so at some of the organizations that I  think you've worked with  finance to fund low-cost schools I'm in  Ghana and can you tell us a little bit  more about that and if you think that  there are other kinds of institutions  that microfinance could partner with  synergistically that way in the future  that's a great question I so our program  that we are working on in Ghana is  basically using microfinance and  microfinance products to improve schools  and so we work with this kind of little  known sector of these low-cost private  schools that actually focus on deprived  communities so where they're either is  no government school or the government  school is too far away or it's over  crowded you know members of the  community just sort of set up their own  school and they charge a you know an  affordable tuition to the parents these  are basically not for-profit schools and  so there are some for-profit schools but  we focus on not for-profit schools and  then you know they use that tuition to  pay the teachers and run the school but  technically they're a business and so  like any business they might need a loan  to grow because even though their  schools they don't get any government  support because they're technically  private so what we part we partnered  with an m fi and Ghana to try to create  a program that would make it easier to  lend to these schools because  microfinance institutions tend not to  like to lend to schools they're tricky  loan clients they don't fit well in a  group blending model all of these  reasons and so we developed and then  tested these different loan products and  so there are things like a lot of  proprietors want a loan to buy a school  bus so that they can transport the  children they can get further out and  get kids in more rural areas so it  increases their enrollment and then they  can also rent that bust out during the  school day so it's an income-generating  activity for the school as well and so  and what we liked about using  microfinance  is when you go into these regions you  know extremely rural areas and  sub-saharan Africa you can get  microfinance can go there a lot of other  interventions can't really go very deep  and very far but when you have a loan  officer looking over their loan  portfolio and their school is in that  loan portfolio they're going to go and  monitor that school they're going to  tell you if teachers aren't showing up  they're going to tell you if because if  the school can't repay the loan it's on  their back so that that basically that  feedback mechanism of microfinance is  very well managed much better manage  than the government circulating amongst  the schools and so we thought well let's  let's use that delivery method to bring  some better education into the schools  as well but berichten did you find that  people were surprised that they are  private schools for lower income folks  operating in Africa as a model it's been  such an uphill battle to explain this  sector to people because as soon as you  say the word private school particularly  to somebody from from the West  immediately they don't wanna hear about  it they're like nope school and the  other thing is is that the big debate is  should educate like is free education or  right and we can sure okay it's a right  that it's whose can pay for that  actually somebody's going to pay for it  it's either our tax dollars you know or  coming so what's the the better question  is not should education be free but what  is the most cost efficient form of  education and there have been studies  that show that these low-cost private  schools are cheaper per student for the  same quality of education as the  government-run student schools and so if  that's true then let's use  that delivery method you know or some  some hybrid partnership of a public  private education model
 

